! Water wB97X / STO-3G: range-separated hybrid regression  (v0.88.0)
!
! Phase-1 wB97 family: single-point energy with the two-exchange Fock
!     F = H + 2J - alpha*K - beta*K_lr(omega) + Vxc ,
! exact-exchange kernel c(r12) = alpha + beta*erf(omega r12) with
! alpha = 0.157706, beta = 0.842294, omega = 0.3 bohr^-1
! [J.-D. Chai, M. Head-Gordon, JCP 128, 084106 (2008)].
!
! Reference values (1 thread; bit-reproducibility references on THIS
! code's grids -- see the note below):
!
!   default grid (medium, MK radial + pruning):
!       E(total) = -75.306671842273 Ha        <-- this input
!   %grid ultrafine (Gaussian UltraFine, EM radial 99x590):
!       E(total) = -75.306725598014 Ha
!   Independent PySCF/libxc oracle at its own grid limit:
!       E(total) = -75.3067213(4)   Ha
!
! NOTE ON GRIDS (a good teaching point, quantified in
! BENCHMARK_NOTES_v0.88.0.md): the cheap MK+pruned presets carry
! O(1e-4) XC-quadrature error for EVERY functional (B3LYP included);
! the EM/TA-based presets (%grid ultrafine / tm / orca7) agree with
! the oracle to a few 1e-6 and bracket it.  The default-grid number
! above is therefore a REPRODUCIBILITY reference, not a converged
! energy -- exactly like the other regression tests of this suite.
!
! The companion validation battery (independent python erf-integral
! reference, omega-limit identities, an omega scan against PySCF with
! %rs_omega, UKS radicals, and the O2 symmetry-breaking note with the
! %scf_converger damping recipe) is documented in
! BENCHMARK_NOTES_v0.88.0.md.
%method  WB97X
%basis   sto-3g
%job     energy
%charge  0
%mult    1
%geom angstrom
O    0.000000    0.000000    0.117300
H    0.000000    0.757200   -0.469200
H    0.000000   -0.757200   -0.469200
*end
